H2: Known Faults - Honda ZR-V
H3: Android Auto and Slow Menus
P: MONITOR ú 2023 on ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says it struggled to establish a connection with Android Auto when it tested the ZR-V: it either dropped instantly or failed to connect at all. The magazine also says the system is not the quickest when loading up a different menu or responding to your inputs.
H3: Lane-Assist and Speed-Limit Beeps
P: MINOR ú 2023 on ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says the lane-departure and blind-spot warning beeps are too excitable for its liking. In Top Gear's long-term test, little bongs chimed every time the driver went even a little over what the ZR-V thought was the speed limit. Parkers' long-term test car produced near-continual beeping when driving down a country road.
H3: Slowing for Lane-Changers
P: MINOR ú 2023 on ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express's long-term ZR-V Sport had a tendency to slow down far too much in response to vehicles changing lanes. The report answers the question "Any problems?" with "None so far".
H3: Regen and Wheelspin Quirks
P: MINOR ú 2023 on ú Get a quote
P: Top Gear says that in most drive modes the level of regen resets to nil every time you press the accelerator. Parkers says that almost every time, its long-term Honda has produced a little chirrup of wheelspin.
H3: DVSA Safety Recalls
P: DVSA's recall data lists a ZR-V recall:
LI: The positions of the conveyor bar and the seat were not properly managed causing interference between the conveyor bar and the headrest.
P: Use our recall check to see whether a car has an open recall.
